---
/**
 * YUNGPRIME case study (copy V1): coordinated Amazon content system for slip-resistant work shoes, May → June 2026.
 * Figures are observed Amazon US account metrics, not a controlled test of the creative. Decisions taken from the copy:
 *  - total sales growth is shown as "+14.1% as reported", with no precise May total (the channel figures add up to
 *    $39,165, i.e. +13.9%); May ACoS is derived from the June ACoS and its reported decrease;
 *  - the Prime Day-adjusted comparisons are left out until their dates and method are validated;
 *  - the CTR example is one secondary ASIN, never account-wide; Share It Studio's scope here is creative, not ads.
 * TODO: confirm when the new creative actually went live (the source shows 2024 folder dates against 2026 data);
 * before/after captures of the Brand Story, Storefront, copy and video.
 */
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/ui/BarChart.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/ui/BeforeAfter.astro';
import { getCaseStudy } from '../../data/caseStudies';
import heroImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/YungPrime Black Slip-Resistant Work Shoes Hero Image_1 (1).jpg';
import detailsImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/YungPrime Black Slip-Resistant Work Shoes Infographic_3.jpg';
import lifestyleImage1 from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/YungPrime Black Slip-Resistant Work Shoes Lifestyle_2 (1).jpg';
import lifestyleImage2 from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/YungPrime Black Slip-Resistant Work Shoes Lifestyle_3 (1).jpg';
import aPlusImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/YungPrime-Premium-A+-Content_06.png';
import beforeMainImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/before/Screenshot 2026-11-04 115922.png';
import beforeDetailImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/before/Screenshot 2026-11-04 115945.png';
import beforeAPlusImage from '../../assets/case-studies/yungprime/before/Screenshot 2026-11-04 120007.png';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';

const cs = getCaseStudy('yungprime');

const metaTitle = 'YUNGPRIME Amazon Case Study: +28.3% Organic Sales | Share It Studio';
const description =
  'Explore YUNGPRIME’s Amazon content redesign across listing images, A+ Content, Storefront, video and copy. June organic sales rose 28.3% in the reported period.';

const challenges = [
  {
    title: 'A fragmented product story.',
    text: 'Listing images, A+ Content, Brand Story, and Storefront were not consistently reinforcing the same value proposition.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Features without enough context.',
    text: 'Important benefits competed for attention instead of addressing shopper questions and objections in a useful order.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Too much dependence on paid reach.',
    text: 'YUNGPRIME needed its Amazon experience to work harder for the traffic it already attracted, not just more ads to send additional visitors to the same disconnected content.',
  },
];

const approach = [
  { title: 'Review the market.', text: 'Examine competitor listings, category expectations, and visual gaps.' },
  {
    title: 'Listen to customer signals.',
    text: 'Use reviews, recurring concerns, and purchasing objections to understand what shoppers still needed to know.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Prioritize the message.',
    text: 'Decide what should be understood first, second, and third — rather than giving every feature equal visual weight.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Connect every touchpoint.',
    text: 'Give listing images, A+ Content, Brand Story, Storefront, copy, and video a distinct role within one creative direction.',
  },
];

const shopperQuestions = [
  'What is it?',
  'Why is it different?',
  'Will it solve my problem?',
  'Can I trust it?',
  'Is it right for my use case?',
];

const deliverables = [
  {
    title: 'Main listing images',
    text: 'Create a more focused first impression and clearer visual presentation of the product.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Additional gallery images',
    text: 'Use infographics and supporting visuals to address benefits, concerns, and real-world use cases in sequence.',
  },
  {
    title: 'A+ Content',
    text: 'Build a deeper explanation below the listing: reinforce the main benefit, address the leading objection, demonstrate quality, show use, and close with confidence.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Brand Story',
    text: 'Introduce YUNGPRIME’s positioning and product philosophy in a recognizable brand narrative.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Amazon Storefront',
    text: 'Organize the broader product range into a more consistent branded shopping destination.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Titles and bullet points',
    text: 'Replace generic, feature-led wording with clearer product benefits and search-relevant language aligned to shopper intent.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Product video',
    text: 'Demonstrate the shoes in use and make functional benefits easier to see than they would be in static imagery alone.',
  },
];

// Original listing creative next to the redesign (Brand Story, Storefront, copy and video have no captures yet).
const comparisons = [
  {
    title: 'Main image',
    before: [
      {
        src: beforeMainImage,
        alt: 'Original main image: a pair of black work clogs, top and sole, on a white background',
      },
    ],
    after: [
      {
        src: heroImage,
        alt: 'Redesigned main image: black slip-resistant work shoes showing the orange slip-resistant sole',
      },
    ],
    stacked: false,
  },
  {
    title: 'Product details',
    before: [{ src: beforeDetailImage, alt: 'Original image: front view of a black work clog' }],
    after: [
      {
        src: detailsImage,
        alt: 'Redesigned image: “Designed with purpose, styled for professionals” exploded view of the shoe',
      },
    ],
    stacked: false,
  },
  {
    title: 'A+ Content',
    before: [
      { src: beforeAPlusImage, alt: 'Original A+ Content banner: “Various color options” above a row of clogs' },
    ],
    after: [{ src: aPlusImage, alt: 'Redesigned Premium A+ Content for YUNGPRIME work shoes' }],
    stacked: true,
  },
  {
    title: 'More from the redesigned gallery',
    before: [],
    after: [
      {
        src: lifestyleImage1,
        alt: 'Redesigned lifestyle image: “Premium EVA material”, a chef holding the shoe',
      },
      {
        src: lifestyleImage2,
        alt: 'Redesigned lifestyle image: “Superior waterproof and oilproof protection” with a water splash',
      },
    ],
    stacked: false,
  },
];

// Amazon US account, May vs. June 2026. Source: the organic performance report supplied by the client.
const metrics = [
  { metric: 'Organic sales', may: '$16,432', june: '$21,077', change: '+28.3%' },
  { metric: 'Paid sales', may: '$22,733', june: '$23,516', change: '+3.4%' },
  { metric: 'Total sales', may: '~$39.2K', june: '$44,593', change: '+14.1%*' },
  { metric: 'Organic share of sales', may: '42.0%', june: '47.3%', change: '+5.3 pp' },
  { metric: 'Return on ad spend (ROAS)', may: '2.63×', june: '2.81×', change: '+6.8%' },
  { metric: 'Advertising cost of sales (ACoS)', may: '37.95%**', june: '35.65%', change: '−2.30 pp' },
  { metric: 'Paid conversion rate', may: '3.46%', june: '3.88%', change: '+0.42 pp' },
  { metric: 'Organic conversion rate', may: '2.74%', june: '2.96%', change: '+0.22 pp' },
];

const organicChart = [
  { label: 'May 2026', value: 16432, text: '$16,432' },
  { label: 'June 2026', value: 21077, text: '$21,077' },
];
const paidChart = [
  { label: 'May 2026', value: 22733, text: '$22,733' },
  { label: 'June 2026', value: 23516, text: '$23,516' },
];
const shareChart = [
  { label: 'May 2026', value: 42.0, text: '42.0%' },
  { label: 'June 2026', value: 47.3, text: '47.3%' },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  kind="case-study"
  caseStudy={cs}
  title="YUNGPRIME: +28.3% Organic Amazon Sales in June"
  highlight="+28.3% Organic Amazon Sales"
  lead="One connected Amazon content system. A stronger month for organic growth."
  metaTitle={metaTitle}
  description={description}
  tags={['May – Jun 2026', 'Amazon US']}
  service={{ label: 'Amazon Listing Optimization', href: '/services/amazon-listing-optimization' }}
  cta={{
    title: 'Is your Amazon content telling one clear story?',
    text: 'Your listing images, A+ Content, Brand Story, Storefront, video, and copy do not have to work in isolation. Show us your product and what shoppers may be missing; we’ll help you discuss the creative priorities worth addressing.',
    ctaLabel: 'Talk to a Human',
    altLabel: 'Explore Amazon Listing Optimization',
    altHref: '/services/amazon-listing-optimization',
  }}
>
  <div class="article-body">
    <aside>
      <p>
        <strong>In short:</strong> YUNGPRIME sells non-slip work shoes designed for demanding workdays. The product had
        an established review base, but its Amazon visuals, product information, A+ Content, and brand experience were
        not telling one clear story. Share It Studio developed a coordinated creative system — from listing images and
        copy to A+ Content, video, Brand Story, and Storefront — to help shoppers understand the product’s value across
        the Amazon buying journey.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Results reported for Amazon US, June vs. May 2026:</strong> <strong>+28.3%</strong> organic sales
        ($16,432 → $21,077), <strong>+14.1%</strong> total sales as reported ($44,593 in June),{' '}
        <strong>2.81× ROAS</strong> (up from 2.63×), and <strong>−2.85%</strong> advertising spend while sales
        increased.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm italic">
        Performance data reflects YUNGPRIME’s Amazon US account in May–June 2026. These results occurred alongside the
        creative work described below; the reports do not isolate the creative changes as the sole cause of growth.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section id="the-challenge">
      <h2>The product was strong. The content was not doing enough.</h2>
      <p>
        YUNGPRIME had a competitive product and a strong review base in a category where shoppers need practical
        answers: How reliable is the traction? Will the shoes feel comfortable through a long shift? Are they easy to
        clean? And what makes them worth choosing over similar-looking options?
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The issue wasn’t a lack of Amazon content. It was how that content worked together.</strong>
      </p>
      {challenges.map((item) => (
        <p>
          <strong>{item.title}</strong>
          {item.text}
        </p>
      ))}
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <strong>The opportunity:</strong> Make the content a connected decision-making experience, not a collection of
          unrelated assets.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section id="our-approach">
      <h2>We started with buyer questions, not new graphics.</h2>
      <p>
        Before building creative, the Share It Studio team worked through four connected steps. Read more about our
        <a href={withBase('/services/amazon-listing-optimization')}>Amazon listing optimization</a> process.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps-list">
        {approach.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.text}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p>The listing’s image sequence was rebuilt around five shopper questions:</p>
      <p>
        <strong>{shopperQuestions.join(' → ')}</strong>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="creative-transformation">
      <h2>Seven deliverable categories. One coherent product story.</h2>
      <ol class="steps-list">
        {deliverables.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.text}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      {comparisons.map((item) => (
        <BeforeAfter title={item.title} before={item.before} after={item.after} stacked={item.stacked} />
      ))}
      <p>
        See our <a href={withBase('/services/amazon-listing-images')}>listing image design</a>,
        <a href={withBase('/services/amazon-a-plus-content')}>A+ Content design</a>,
        <a href={withBase('/services/amazon-brand-story-design')}>Brand Story design</a> and
        <a href={withBase('/services/amazon-storefront-design')}>Storefront design</a> services.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="results">
      <h2>Organic sales grew faster than paid sales.</h2>
      <p>
        YUNGPRIME reported <strong>$21,077 in organic Amazon sales in June</strong>, up 28.3% from $16,432 in May. Paid
        sales increased more modestly, from $22,733 to $23,516 (+3.4%), while advertising spend declined 2.85%.
      </p>
      <p>
        The account recorded <strong>$44,593 in total June sales</strong>, with a 14.1% month-over-month increase as
        reported. The organic share of sales grew from <strong>42.0% to 47.3%</strong> — a 5.3-percentage-point shift
        toward organic revenue.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption class="sr-only">Amazon US account metrics, May vs. June 2026</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Metric</th>
              <th scope="col">May 2026</th>
              <th scope="col">June 2026</th>
              <th scope="col">Reported change</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {metrics.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.metric}</th>
                <td>{row.may}</td>
                <td>{row.june}</td>
                <td>{row.change}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm italic">
        * The source report states +14.1% total sales growth. The displayed rounded and channel-level May figures do not
        exactly reconcile to that percentage; confirm the authoritative unrounded baseline before publishing a precise
        May total. ** The May ACoS is calculated from the reported June ACoS and 2.30-pp decrease.
      </p>
      <div class="my-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <BarChart title="Organic sales" bars={organicChart} />
        <BarChart title="Paid sales" bars={paidChart} />
        <BarChart title="Organic share of sales" bars={shareChart} />
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>1,048 units sold in June — up 13.9% from May.</strong>
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm italic">
        These are observed account metrics, not an isolated experiment attributing all performance changes to creative
        assets.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="conversion-and-efficiency">
      <h2>More of the traffic that arrived went on to buy.</h2>
      <p>YUNGPRIME’s reported conversion rates improved in both channels:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Organic conversion rate:</strong> 2.74% → 2.96%
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ads conversion rate:</strong> 3.46% → 3.88%
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Advertising efficiency moved in the same favorable direction.{' '}
        <strong>ROAS increased from 2.63× to 2.81×</strong>, while{' '}
        <strong>ACoS declined by 2.30 percentage points to 35.65%</strong>. The Buy Box remained approximately 93%,
        giving the team more confidence that the account had not simply lost control of the purchase experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Results also extended beyond the two featured ASINs.</strong> The rest of the catalog reported +13.6% in
        sales, +17.6% in organic sales, and an ROAS increase from 2.91× to 3.13×. This suggests broader account
        momentum; it does <strong>not</strong> mean that the featured creative assets caused gains across every product.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-the-data-taught-us">
      <h2>A lower click-through rate did not tell the whole story.</h2>
      <p>
        For one secondary ASIN, <strong>organic impressions rose 56.5%</strong> — from 119,128 to 186,407 — while
        organic CTR fell from 5.95% to 4.74%. On that same ASIN, reported cart additions increased 183% and purchases
        increased 167%.
      </p>
      <p>
        The report’s diagnosis: the product was reaching a broader set of searches, including less-targeted impressions.
        A softer CTR alongside stronger downstream activity was a reason to examine the full journey, not automatically
        label the creative a failure.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          That is the same principle behind our approach to content:
          <strong>
            judge each asset by the question it answers and its place in the customer journey, not by appearance or one
            isolated metric.
          </strong>
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="text-sm italic">These figures describe one secondary ASIN, not the whole account.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="takeaway">
      <h2>The transformation was bigger than a new set of images.</h2>
      <p>
        YUNGPRIME’s project replaced disconnected creative with a coordinated Amazon content system: a clearer listing
        sequence, a deeper A+ explanation, a more recognizable Brand Story, an organized Storefront, better-structured
        copy, and product video with a defined purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        The May–June reporting period also showed stronger organic sales, improved conversion rates, and more efficient
        advertising. The two developments are documented together; the data does not establish how much of the growth
        came specifically from the creative work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The principle:</strong> Make every Amazon touchpoint answer a different shopper question — and make all
        of them tell the same story.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
